you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  yet more records broken as it's revealed  europe experienced its hottest year ever  in 2020  a warning summer holiday trips to  tourist hot spots could be under threat  from climate change  and should beavers be reintroduced to  the wild in england  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show now last year was the  hottest year on record in europe with  the average temperature across the  continent reaching nearly two degrees  more than the long-term average that's  from the latest in an annual series of  global climate reports it shows how  temperatures in europe have been  increasing in recent years reaching a  record level in 2020. now all five of  the warmest years  four annual average temperatures have  occurred since 2014. now when we look at  what that meant for individual countries  many places especially in eastern europe  reported their highest ever yearly  average temperatures in 2020. now russia  was nearly three degrees above the  average from 1981 to 2010 while estonia  finland and ukraine were all above two  degrees warmer  now although parts of western europe  were relatively cooler  countries still recorded temperatures up  to two degrees above the average for the  long-term period and the uk  had its third warmest year since  national records began at an average of  0.8 degrees warmer than normal and the  report also found that globally  atmospheric concentrations of the three  main greenhouse gases carbon dioxide  methane and nitrous oxide reached record  values in 2020 despite lockdowns because  of the covert 19 pandemic  well joining me now is dr robert dunn  senior climate scientist at the met  office who was the lead editor for the  global climate chapter of that report  welcome to you first of all how sure can  we be that human-induced climate change  is responsible for this record average  temperature rise in europe  what we can say is from the recent ipcc  that on a global level  climate ruling climate and extreme um  warmer temperatures  is unequivocally due to human actions  and what we see in europe in 2020 this  1.9 degrees above the baseline average  is part of that picture but it's not the  only thing that's going on  and what are the effects of the  increasing temperatures around the world  what impact do they have  so during 2020 we saw the warmest  temperature recorded since 1931 anywhere  in the globe at 54.4 degrees in death  valley um that was actually then equaled  again likely this year  um globally we see that 2020 was one of  the top three warmest years on record  that's the same as for the uk as you  just said but we see increased  temperatures elsewhere as well so we had  a record temperature of 18.3 degrees on  the continent of antarctica  and actually in the arctic circle we saw  a record temperature of 38 degrees so  we're seeing high temperatures across  the world but we're also seeing the  response of the climate to that so  mountain glaciers are still losing mass  and their ice is melting we're seeing  permafrost temperatures reaching record  values  um  northern hemisphere lakes are covered  with ice for less time 8.5 days fewer  last year but also our biosphere is  responding so colleagues here at the uk  met office studying how oak trees are  responding to the warming temperatures  and their leaves are coming out earlier  and they came into earth earlier  earliest in any time the last 20 years  in 2020  and what about concentrations of  greenhouse gases were you surprised for  example they didn't drop during the  lockdown months  given the kobe pandemic  in the state of the climate report we  see that the  emissions of greenhouse gases in 2020  were a bit lower than what we'd expected  by about six to seven percent  that's a small change but it does show  that  some of the effects of the kobe 19  epidemic and the lockdowns means that  if we act as a global society we can  change how much we emit but by just  reducing the emissions we're still  putting more stuff into the atmosphere  and therefore the um the concentrations  we see are still rising  dr robert dunn thanks very much indeed  thank you let's take a look now at some  of the day's other climate news and  authorities in tennessee say they've  accounted for dozens of people who were  previously listed as missing after  flooding killed at least 21 people in  the u.s state over the weekend  scientists say that climate change will  make extreme weather events like  flooding and wildfires more likely  people won't be able to reduce the  amount of greenhouse gases emitted from  their homes unless the government does  more to help according to consumer  groups they've written to the prime  minister saying it's too expensive  complicated and risky for people to  decarbonize their houses  england's homes produce more carbon  emissions every year than all of the  country's cars according to new research  from the national housing federation  and the united nations patron of the  oceans is beginning to an attempt to  complete the coldest swim on earth lewis  pew is swimming across the 10 kilometer  mouth of the illusion ice fjord which is  fed by the world's fastest moving  glacier he's calling for more nations to  join an initiative to protect 30 of the  world's oceans by 2030 stressing to  world leaders that healthy oceans can  help mitigate climate change  now if you've enjoyed a staycation this  year visiting popular tourist sites in  the uk then this story is for you the  national trust is warning that  traditional summer holiday trips could  be under threat because of climate  change they say that temporary closures  due to flooding and storms as well as  extreme heat could mean visitors need to  adapt their plans in the future well  lizzie carlisle is the national trust  head of climate and environment and she  joins me now welcome to you so how might  uk holidays be affected by climate  change in the future  hi uh well thanks for having me on um i  think what our research has showed is  that um  with the increasing hot days that we're  likely to get  and increasing  downpours and intense periods of  rainfall we will see changing visitor  patterns and and what we've shown  through this research is that uh we  might see a shift in season so the  summer may just become too hot to be our  peak season and that might shift into  autumn  uh but we'll also see i think  visitor surges so when the weather does  get hot  uh we know that people love to go  outdoors into the coast and i think  we're already beginning to see what the  impact of those visitor surges look like  but we're expecting to see  far more of those as as the temperature  rises  and what can you do to protect your  sites and protect the public from some  of the risks associated with climate  change  well i think the first thing to say is  that we haven't got all the answers and  and this research is really trying to  kick off where we should look  i mean certainly we know there are some  everyday things that we can do like  providing shady  uh seating or lovely woodland walks that  will help people enjoy our places  in more extreme climates i think we're  also thinking about  how we hold water back in the catchment  away from our places to avoid flooding  for example  and finally just  engaging with our visitors and  helping them understand how to enjoy our  places without without damaging them so  not littering for example  and siestas for your workers in the  summer  well that's one of the options that  we're we're considering and testing a  couple of places but certainly you know  the same the same uh principles apply to  our visitors as they do our our staff  and volunteers we really want to make  sure that everyone can enjoy our places  and that might mean that our staff and  volunteers  choose to work in slightly different  patterns earlier in the day later in the  day to avoid those  those high  peaks of heat at lunch  um but but it's just one of the measures  that we're looking at to to try and make  sure that we're adapting our places to  climate change  lizzie carlile thanks very much indeed  thank you  they're known as eco engineers and for  many the work beavers do creating  natural wetlands and diverse habitats is  crucial to restoring nature but the  semi-aquatic creatures can cause  significant damage to farmland through  flood damage  now a government consultation will  establish whether beavers should be  officially reintroduced to the wild in  england for the first time in 400 years  as fraser ward reports  here in devon for the past few years a  community of hard-working  environmentalists has been steadily  growing under the watchful eye of local  wildlife groups and the government  no one knows quite how eurasian beavers  were originally released into the river  otter but now defra are starting a  consultation to see if more of these  construction loving rodents can be  reintroduced elsewhere  one of the effects of climate change  will be more extreme weather seen for  example in the form of more  intense rainfall episodes which can  cause flooding and also more droughts  and beavers at both ends of that  spectrum of dry and wet can help us cope  by reducing the flood peaks that go down  rivers after heavy rain and also holding  back water like this wonderful sponge  we're standing in today if it didn't  rain for a long time the river would  still flow because of the work that the  beavers are doing here  the dams which beavers build can raise  water levels and form wetlands these  areas as well as creating habitats for a  wide range of plants and animals are  also thought to be useful environments  for storing carbon potentially reducing  the emission of greenhouse gases that  cause climate change  some landowners and anglers have  concerns about the impact the beavers  may have hence the government's 12-week  consultation beaver's in the right place  can do amazing things  beavers in the wrong place can cause  damage for flooding farms flooding  property  taking out trees  flooding roads and we've seen all that  in this trial so we've learnt a lot  about beavers and the good they can do  but also the need to be ma the need for  management so that the the damage and  risks they cause to property can be uh  mitigated  others see them as natural and effective  ecosystem engineers  most of our rivers and watercourses in  this country are very straightened uh  very canalized what beavers do is they  bring that natural dynamism back so they  create dams and ponds and riffles and  all these are important habitats for  other species so uh in the work we've  done here on this trial we've seen  insects increased we've seen birds  increase we've seen the diversity of  plants increase  those who support the reintroduction of  a species made extinct in the uk back in  the 16th century hope that it'll provide  damning evidence that their return will  have a positive environmental impact  [Applause]  fraser moored sky news  well that's everything from us for today  next time lessons with a difference how  teaching landowners about rewilding  could help boost biodiversity you can  see that here on the daily climate show  tomorrow on sky news see you then  [Music]

